+= matrix[i][j];\n        P[i+1][j+1] = cum_row + P[i][j+1]; // This works because P[i][j+1] already contains sum of rows above up to column j.\nYes, that's correct: at the time we compute (i+1,j+1), P[i][j+1] is already computed from previous row i. So we can do a single double loop with parallelization? But there is dependency across rows because each cell depends on same column in previous row. However, within a row, cells are independent of each other if we have cum_row and P[i][j+1]. Actually for fixed i, as j increases, cum_row accumulates, but P[i][j+1] is from previous row and does not change during the inner loop. So all iterations in the inner loop can be done in parallel? No, because cum_row depends on previous j (sequential). But we could compute row_prefix first per row independently, then do column accumulation separately.\n\nSimpler: Use two passes as described earlier:\nPass 1: For each i in parallel, compute row cumulative sum into a temporary array row_sum[i][j] = sum_{k=0..j} matrix[i][k]. This is independent per row.\nPass 2: Compute integral image P by column-wise accumulation: for j from 0 to n-1 (parallel over columns), for i from 0 to n-1 sequentially, set P[i+1][j+1] = row_sum[i][j] + P[i][j+1]. This works because within a column, each step depends on previous row's P.\n\nBut we can avoid extra storage by using the matrix itself? Not safe. We'll allocate vector<vector<long long>> prefix(n+1, vector<long long>(n+1, 0)). Then do:\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    long long cum = 0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        cum += matrix[i][j];\n        prefix[i+1][j+1] = cum + prefix[i][j+1]; // This uses prefix[i][j+1] which is from previous row. But if we parallelize rows, different threads may read prefix[i][j+1] while it's being written by another thread? Since each i writes to its own row (i+1) and reads from row i, there is a dependency: row i must be fully computed before row i+1 can use it