n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 2: compute integral image P of size (n+1)x(n+1)\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> P(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        long long cum_col = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            cum_col += row_cum[i][j];\n            P[i+1][j+1] = cum_col;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 3: search all top-left corners\n    long long mxSum = -9e18; // or use LLONG_MIN\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n-s; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n-s; ++j) {\n            long long sum = P[i+s][j+s] - P[i][j+s] - P[i+s][j] + P[i][j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n\nBut note: The original output is a 32-bit signed integer, but we use long long for safety. Print with %lld.\n\nWe must ensure that the reduction works correctly: OpenMP reduction(max:mxSum) requires that mxSum be initialized before the parallel region (outside). We set it to LLONG_MIN or -9e18. But better to initialize after reading n,s? Actually we can set mxSum = P[s][s] - 0 - 0 + 0 = P[s][s] as initial value, but reduction will handle.\n\nPotential issue: The integral image computation in step 2 uses row_cum[i][j] which is cumulative sum up to column j. That's correct because we want sum of all rows above and including i for columns <= j. So P[i+1][j+1] = cum_col after processing row i.\n\nCheck correctness with small example: matrix [[3,6],[5,6]], n=2,s=2.\nrow_cum[0]=[3,9]; row_cum[1]=[5,11].\nStep 2:\nj=0: cum_col starts 0; i=0: cum_col+=3 => P[1][1]=3; i=1: cum_col+=5 => P[2][1]=8.\nj=1: cum_col=0; i=0: cum_col+=9 => P[1][2]=9; i=1: cum_col+=11 => P[2][2]=20.\nNow submatrix sum at (0,0) size 2x2 = P[2][2] - P[0][2] - P[2][0] + P[0][0] = 20-0-0+0=20. Actual sum: 3+6+5+6=20. Correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the parallel loops are